{"Split cleanup not atomic: CleanAnnotations loads IDs via SELECT (fetchIDs) and deletes in separate operation (deleteByIDs) without wrapping both actions in a single transaction, breaking atomicity in high-concurrency environments": 0.43356195092201233, "Resource leak: CleanUpService.Run creates a time.Ticker but never calls ticker.Stop() when context is canceled, causing the ticker's underlying goroutine to continue running and leading to goroutine leak": 0.24636013805866241, "Test coverage gaps: Large-batch tests only target SQLite, leaving MySQL/PostgreSQL parameterized DELETE paths unvalidated": 0.12668359279632568, "Tests rely on explicit inserted IDs that may be overwritten by some DB backends, risking flaky or inconsistent test data across environments": 0.19182386994361877, "SQLite large IN clause path risks SQL size and performance limits: when batch size exceeds 999, deleteByIDs inlines all IDs directly into DELETE statement without placeholders, potentially generating very large SQL strings": 0.51488196849823, "fetchIDs builds SQL using dynamic table name and condition with fragility risk: callers pass conditions mixing column filters, ORDER BY, and LIMIT, which could yield invalid SQL if condition format changes or is misconstructed": 0.2225283980369568}